196
00:14:19.059 --> 00:14:22.472
Embeddings match meaning; they represent rare codes poorly.

197
00:14:22.752 --> 00:14:26.528
BM25 matches words; it misses paraphrases.

198
00:14:26.808 --> 00:14:35.064
Reciprocal rank fusion combines the two ranked lists by rank alone: score(d) = Σ 1 / (60 + ranki(d)).

199
00:14:35.344 --> 00:14:41.680
It needs no score calibration between retrievers, which is why it's the default fusion in search engines.

200
00:14:41.960 --> 00:14:46.440
A cross-encoder re-ranker on the fused top 50 is the usual next step.

201
00:14:46.720 --> 00:14:51.883
See Ch 22 for BM25 and HNSW internals.

202
00:14:53.233 --> 00:14:54.022
Serving:

203
00:14:54.302 --> 00:14:56.905
TTFT, throughput and the KV cache.

204
00:14:57.185 --> 00:14:59.211
Generating text has two phases:

205
00:14:59.491 --> 00:15:01.817
Prefill reads the whole prompt in parallel.

206
00:15:02.097 --> 00:15:05.126
It's compute-bound and determines time to first token.

207
00:15:05.406 --> 00:15:09.502
Decode produces one token per step for every sequence in the batch.

208
00:15:09.782 --> 00:15:19.382
Each step must read all the weights from GPU memory, so it's memory-bandwidth-bound: a batch of 100 costs little more per step than a batch of 1.

209
00:15:19.662 --> 00:15:21.774
Batching is where the throughput comes from.

210
00:15:23.124 --> 00:15:28.009
Each sequence keeps the keys and values of every token it has seen: the KV cache.

211
00:15:28.289 --> 00:15:41.601
For an 8B model with grouped-query attention that is about 128 KB per token, so a 3,000-token conversation needs ~375 MB of GPU memory.

212
00:15:41.881 --> 00:15:45.209
KV memory, not compute, often caps concurrency.

