GPUs for 180 req per second:

233
00:16:59.743 --> 00:17:00.533
20.

234
00:17:01.883 --> 00:17:11.312
Continuous (iteration-level) batching admits new requests and retires finished ones at every decode step, instead of waiting for a whole batch to finish.

235
00:17:11.592 --> 00:17:21.043
Paged KV (PagedAttention) allocates KV memory in small blocks as a sequence grows, instead of reserving the maximum context up front.

236
00:17:21.323 --> 00:17:28.619
Prefix caching stores the KV of the shared system prompt once and reuses it, skipping its prefill on every request.

237
00:17:28.899 --> 00:17:36.195
These are what serving engines such as vLLM, TGI, TensorRT-LLM and SGLang implement.

238
00:17:37.545 --> 00:17:38.782
Caching and routing.

239
00:17:39.062 --> 00:17:40.683
Two levers cut the model bill.

240
00:17:40.963 --> 00:17:42.862
Both can silently hurt quality.

241
00:17:43.142 --> 00:17:44.315
Response caching.

242
00:17:44.595 --> 00:17:46.387
Exact-match caching is safe.

243
00:17:46.667 --> 00:18:03.713
Semantic caching (reuse the answer to a similar question) sounds better, but L3 found that the toy embedding puts paraphrases of one question at 0.60 to 0.78 similarity and another customer's order number at 0.99.

244
00:18:03.993 --> 00:18:10.670
No threshold separates them, and a semantic cache served wrong answers for 22 to 40% of questions.

245
00:18:10.950 --> 00:18:21.403
Key the cache by what the answer depends on (intent + extracted entities + user scope), cache only non-personal answers, and set TTLs by data freshness.

246
00:18:22.753 --> 00:18:23.777
Model routing.

247
00:18:24.057 --> 00:18:25.529
Most questions are easy.

248
00:18:25.809 --> 00:18:29.223
A small model costs ~25x less per token.

249
00:18:29.503 --> 00:18:37.076
Route by detected complexity and escalate when a cheap verifier or the small model's own confidence says it is unsure:

250
00:18:37.356 --> 00:18:46.167
L3 measured $0.96 versus $8.10 per 1,000 answers at the same overall quality.

251
00:18:47.517 --> 00:18:49.842
Agents and tools: the policy layer.

252
00:18:50.122 --> 00:18:58.549
When the assistant can call issuerefund, the model's output becomes a request to move money, and the model can be wrong, tricked, or retried.

253
00:18:58.829 --> 00:19:01.346
Treat every tool call as untrusted input:

254
00:19:01.626 --> 00:19:03.930
Intent from the user, not from context.

255
00:19:04.210 --> 00:19:07.879
Which tools exist in this turn depends on the user's own words.

256
00:19:08.159 --> 00:19:16.309
Retrieved text (reviews, emails, web pages) can contain indirect prompt injection, and is never allowed to enable tools.

257
00:19:16.589 --> 00:19:18.466
Arguments checked against the session.

258
00:19:18.746 --> 00:19:24.378
The order id must belong to this user; the amount must be within policy and what was paid.

259
00:19:25.728 --> 00:19:31.872
Idempotency keys on every side effect, so a retried timeout cannot pay twice (Ch 20).

260
00:19:32.152 --> 00:19:40.237
Confirmation for money-moving or irreversible actions, rendered by the UI from the validated call, not from the model's prose.

261
00:19:40.517 --> 00:19:45.701
Least privilege: tools run with the user's permissions, never a service-admin token.

