651
00:48:45.625 --> 00:48:46.499
Part 8.

652
00:48:46.779 --> 00:48:48.123
Interview Q and A.

653
00:48:49.373 --> 00:48:50.205
Beginner.

654
00:48:50.485 --> 00:48:51.509
Question 1.

655
00:48:51.789 --> 00:48:56.547
Design a ChatGPT-style assistant for a grocery app, main components?

656
00:48:56.827 --> 00:48:58.192
Thirty-second answer:

657
00:48:58.472 --> 00:49:15.176
A chat gateway (auth, rate limits, streaming), then an orchestrator that detects intent, retrieves context with hybrid search, assembles the prompt and runs the tool loop, then a model gateway that routes, caches and falls back across self-hosted and provider models,

658
00:49:15.456 --> 00:49:25.376
then an inference fleet with continuous batching, then a policy layer in front of order and refund APIs; plus ingestion, evaluation and observability.

659
00:49:26.726 --> 00:49:27.729
Question 2.

660
00:49:28.009 --> 00:49:31.145
What is RAG and why not just fine-tune the model?

661
00:49:31.425 --> 00:49:32.790
Thirty-second answer:

662
00:49:33.070 --> 00:49:44.227
Retrieval-augmented generation looks up relevant documents at question time and puts them in the prompt, so answers are grounded, citable and update the moment the data changes.

663
00:49:44.507 --> 00:49:50.609
Fine-tuning teaches style or skills, not fresh facts like today's price or this customer's order.

664
00:49:51.959 --> 00:49:53.004
Question 3.

665
00:49:53.284 --> 00:49:54.969
How do you choose the chunk size?

666
00:49:55.249 --> 00:49:56.615
Thirty-second answer:

667
00:49:56.895 --> 00:50:03.743
Measure it: on judged questions, check that the complete answer lands in the retrieved chunks, against prompt tokens.

668
00:50:04.023 --> 00:50:17.463
Tiny chunks split answers (67% complete in L1), huge ones double the tokens; ~120 words with overlap and the title on each chunk gave 93% at half the tokens.

669
00:50:18.813 --> 00:50:19.879
Question 4.

670
00:50:20.159 --> 00:50:24.170
What do TTFT and tokens per second mean, and which matters more?

671
00:50:24.450 --> 00:50:25.815
Thirty-second answer:

672
00:50:26.095 --> 00:50:34.714
Time to first token is how long the user waits before text appears (prefill plus queueing); tokens per second is how fast the answer streams.

673
00:50:34.994 --> 00:50:44.338
For chat, TTFT p99 is the felt latency; streaming speed only needs to beat reading speed (~20 tokens per second).

674
00:50:45.688 --> 00:50:46.797
Question 5.

675
00:50:47.077 --> 00:50:48.549
Why stream responses?

676
00:50:48.829 --> 00:50:50.195
Thirty-second answer:

677
00:50:50.475 --> 00:51:03.637
A 220-token answer takes several seconds to generate; streaming shows the first words in ~0.1 to 2 s, so perceived latency is TTFT rather than total time.

678
00:51:03.917 --> 00:51:09.101
Use SSE or WebSockets through the gateway, and render citations as they arrive.

679
00:51:10.451 --> 00:51:11.454
Intermediate.

680
00:51:11.734 --> 00:51:12.843
Question 6.

681
00:51:13.123 --> 00:51:15.747
Why hybrid search instead of only vector search?

682
00:51:16.027 --> 00:51:17.393
Thirty-second answer:

683
00:51:17.673 --> 00:51:32.307
Embeddings match meaning but represent rare tokens poorly, so SKU codes and order ids vanish (0% in L1); BM25 matches words exactly but misses paraphrases (55%).

684
00:51:32.587 --> 00:51:38.646
Reciprocal rank fusion combines the two rankings without calibrating scores and keeps both strengths.

685
00:51:39.996 --> 00:51:41.084
Question 7.

686
00:51:41.364 --> 00:51:43.625
How do you evaluate a RAG system?

687
00:51:43.905 --> 00:51:45.271
Thirty-second answer:

688
00:51:45.551 --> 00:52:05.817
Separately evaluate retrieval (recall@k, MRR and whether the complete answer is in context, on judged questions) and generation (facts present, citations valid, graded by rules and a calibrated LLM judge), plus safety suites; gate every prompt, model and index change on them, and watch online feedback.

689
00:52:07.167 --> 00:52:08.191
Question 8.

690
00:52:08.471 --> 00:52:11.650
What is the KV cache and why does it limit concurrency?

691
00:52:11.930 --> 00:52:13.295
Thirty-second answer:

692
00:52:13.575 --> 00:52:29.917
During generation each sequence keeps the keys and values for every token it has seen, about 128 KB per token for an 8B model, so a 3,000-token conversation holds ~375 MB of GPU memory.

693
00:52:30.197 --> 00:52:34.293
The memory left after the weights caps how many sequences can be in the batch.

694
00:52:35.643 --> 00:52:36.709
Question 9.

695
00:52:36.989 --> 00:52:39.101
How do you handle a provider outage?

696
00:52:39.381 --> 00:52:40.747
Thirty-second answer:

697
00:52:41.027 --> 00:52:58.413
A model gateway with health checks and fallback: route to a second provider or a self-hosted model with the same prompt contract, degrade to a smaller model or to plain search results, and tell the user what can't be done right now; never retry storms against a failing API.

698
00:52:59.763 --> 00:53:00.531
Senior.

699
00:53:00.811 --> 00:53:01.857
Question 10.

700
00:53:02.137 --> 00:53:04.035
How do you size the GPU fleet?

701
00:53:04.315 --> 00:53:05.681
Thirty-second answer:

702
00:53:05.961 --> 00:53:23.966
From the peak request rate and the per-GPU capacity at the TTFT SLO under your scheduler and token mix: in L2, 180 req per second needs 90 GPUs with KV reservation, 26 with paged KV and 20 with a prefix cache too.

703
00:53:24.246 --> 00:53:31.670
Measure capacity with your real prompt and output lengths, then add headroom and warm spares because model loading takes minutes.

704
00:53:33.020 --> 00:53:34.151
Question 11.

705
00:53:34.431 --> 00:53:37.481
Explain continuous batching and paged attention.

706
00:53:37.761 --> 00:53:39.127
Thirty-second answer:

707
00:53:39.407 --> 00:53:49.945
Continuous batching schedules at every decode step: finished sequences leave and waiting ones join immediately, instead of a batch running until its slowest member finishes.

708
00:53:50.225 --> 00:54:01.575
Paged attention allocates KV memory in small blocks as sequences grow instead of reserving the maximum context, so more sequences fit and fragmentation disappears.

709
00:54:02.925 --> 00:54:04.013
Question 12.

710
00:54:04.293 --> 00:54:05.893
Should we add a semantic cache?

711
00:54:06.173 --> 00:54:07.538
Thirty-second answer:

712
00:54:07.818 --> 00:54:10.975
Only with great care: similarity is not equality.

713
00:54:11.255 --> 00:54:24.290
In L3, paraphrases scored 0.60 to 0.78 while another customer's order scored 0.99, so every threshold served 22 to 40% wrong answers.

714
00:54:24.570 --> 00:54:34.554
Cache exact matches, or key the cache by intent plus extracted entities plus user scope, never share personal answers, and set TTLs by freshness.

715
00:54:35.904 --> 00:54:37.184
Question 13.

716
00:54:37.464 --> 00:54:40.344
How do you cut the model bill without losing quality?

717
00:54:40.624 --> 00:54:41.989
Thirty-second answer:

718
00:54:42.269 --> 00:55:05.672
Shorter prompts (right-sized chunks, capped context), prefix caching, keyed response caching for common non-personal questions, routing easy turns to a small model with escalation (L3: $0.96 versus $8.10 per 1,000 answers at the same quality), output length caps, and per-conversation budgets with graceful degradation.

719
00:55:07.022 --> 00:55:08.217
Question 14.

720
00:55:08.497 --> 00:55:10.182
How do you keep the index fresh?

721
00:55:10.462 --> 00:55:11.827
Thirty-second answer:

722
00:55:12.107 --> 00:55:31.521
Drive ingestion from change events (CDC from the catalogue and CMS), re-chunk and re-embed only changed documents, write to a new index version and swap atomically for model or chunking changes, and look up volatile facts like price and stock live through tools rather than from the index.

723
00:55:32.871 --> 00:55:33.767
Architect.

724
00:55:34.047 --> 00:55:35.241
Question 15.

725
00:55:35.521 --> 00:55:38.359
How do you stop prompt injection from causing harm?

726
00:55:38.639 --> 00:55:40.004
Thirty-second answer:

727
00:55:40.284 --> 00:55:59.804
Assume it will succeed at the model and contain the damage: tools are enabled by the user's detected intent (never by retrieved text), every argument is checked against what this user owns and the policy, side effects need a UI-rendered confirmation and an idempotency key, and tools run with the user's permissions.

728
00:56:00.084 --> 00:56:05.695
In L4 those layers took unauthorized actions from 527 to 0.

729
00:56:07.045 --> 00:56:08.239
Question 16.

730
00:56:08.519 --> 00:56:10.034
Build or buy the model?

731
00:56:10.314 --> 00:56:11.679
Thirty-second answer:

732
00:56:11.959 --> 00:56:25.079
Both behind a model gateway: providers for frontier quality, spiky traffic and fallback; self-hosted open-weights models for high-volume easy traffic, cost control, latency tuning and data residency.

733
00:56:25.359 --> 00:56:31.226
Decide per use case with cost per 1,000 answers at the quality bar, and keep prompts portable.

734
00:56:32.576 --> 00:56:33.920
Question 17.

735
00:56:34.200 --> 00:56:36.824
What must never happen, and how do you guarantee it?

736
00:56:37.104 --> 00:56:38.469
Thirty-second answer:

737
00:56:38.749 --> 00:56:57.395
Money moved without intent or twice (policy layer, idempotency), one customer's data shown to another (no shared personal caches, user-scoped tools), confident answers that contradict policy (grounding, citations, eval gates), and an outage taking the whole assistant down (fallbacks).

738
00:56:57.675 --> 00:57:00.256
Each has a test in the eval and safety suites.

739
00:57:01.606 --> 00:57:02.779
Question 18.

740
00:57:03.059 --> 00:57:07.177
How do you design multi-step agents (plan, call tools, reflect)?

741
00:57:07.457 --> 00:57:08.822
Thirty-second answer:

742
00:57:09.102 --> 00:57:26.190
As a bounded state machine: a maximum number of steps and tokens per turn, each step's tool call validated by the policy layer, intermediate results kept in the conversation store, idempotent side effects, and a human handoff when the plan exceeds its budget or confidence.

743
00:57:26.470 --> 00:57:31.910
Prefer deterministic workflows for known tasks and agents only where flexibility pays.

744
00:57:33.260 --> 00:57:34.497
Question 19.

745
00:57:34.777 --> 00:57:37.487
How do privacy and compliance shape the design?

746
00:57:37.767 --> 00:57:39.132
Thirty-second answer:

747
00:57:39.412 --> 00:57:58.356
Minimise and redact personal data in prompts, keep prompts and logs in-region where the law requires, set retention limits, honour deletion across the conversation store, logs, caches and eval sets, document which providers process what, and never train on customer conversations without consent.

748
00:57:59.706 --> 00:58:00.815
Question 20.

749
00:58:01.095 --> 00:58:03.314
What makes an AI assistant trustworthy?

750
00:58:03.594 --> 00:58:04.959
Thirty-second answer:

751
00:58:05.239 --> 00:58:23.458
Grounded answers with citations from well-measured retrieval, predictable latency from a well-scheduled fleet, cost controls that never trade away correctness or privacy, and a policy layer that makes the model unable to do harm on its own, all gated by evaluation on every change.

752
00:58:24.808 --> 00:58:25.704
Part 9.

753
00:58:25.984 --> 00:58:27.392
Summary and Practice.

754
00:58:28.642 --> 00:58:29.730
Key takeaways.

755
00:58:30.010 --> 00:58:32.207
Retrieval decides what the model knows.

756
00:58:32.487 --> 00:58:42.471
Hybrid BM25 + vectors with RRF, ~120-word chunks with titles, measured by complete answer in context (L1).

757
00:58:42.751 --> 00:58:44.522
Serving decides what it costs.

758
00:58:44.802 --> 00:58:54.061
Continuous batching, paged KV and prefix caching changed the fleet from 90 to 20 GPUs for the same model and SLO (L2).

759
00:58:54.341 --> 00:58:56.303
Similarity is not equality.

760
00:58:56.583 --> 00:59:04.157
Cache by intent + entities + scope, never by embedding distance alone, and never share personal answers (L3).

761
00:59:04.437 --> 00:59:08.469
Route most traffic to a small model and escalate the hard tail (L3).

762
00:59:09.819 --> 00:59:12.037
The model is not the security boundary.

763
00:59:12.317 --> 00:59:18.035
Intent from the user, ownership and policy checks, idempotency and confirmation (L4).

764
00:59:18.315 --> 00:59:22.112
Evaluation is the test suite for prompts, models and indexes.

765
00:59:23.462 --> 00:59:24.785
Decision cheat sheet.

766
00:59:25.065 --> 00:59:26.409
Here is a quick table:

767
00:59:26.689 --> 00:59:27.862
Need, and Choice.

768
00:59:28.142 --> 00:59:29.038
Retrieval:

769
00:59:29.318 --> 00:59:38.726
Hybrid BM25 + vectors, RRF k=60, re-ranker; ~120-word chunks with overlap + titles.

770
00:59:39.006 --> 00:59:39.881
Freshness:

771
00:59:40.161 --> 00:59:44.107
CDC-driven re-indexing; live tools for price and stock.

772
00:59:44.387 --> 00:59:45.177
Serving:

773
00:59:45.457 --> 00:59:51.409
Continuous batching + paged KV + prefix cache; autoscale on queue depth and KV.

774
00:59:51.689 --> 00:59:52.947
Latency SLO:

775
00:59:53.227 --> 00:59:56.214
TTFT p99, streamed responses.

776
00:59:56.494 --> 00:59:57.326
Caching:

777
00:59:57.606 --> 01:00:02.427
Exact match + keyed (intent, entities, scope); no semantic answer cache.

778
01:00:02.707 --> 01:00:03.433
Cost:

779
01:00:03.713 --> 01:00:07.553
Small-model routing with escalation; budgets per conversation.

780
01:00:07.833 --> 01:00:08.707
Tools:

781
01:00:08.987 --> 01:00:13.787
Intent allow-list from user, ownership checks, idempotency keys, confirmation.

782
01:00:14.067 --> 01:00:15.049
Resilience:

783
01:00:15.329 --> 01:00:18.529
Model gateway with fallbacks; degrade to search results.

784
01:00:19.879 --> 01:00:20.689
Quality:

785
01:00:20.969 --> 01:00:24.468
Judged eval sets gating every change; online feedback.

786
01:00:25.818 --> 01:00:26.949
Exercises.

787
01:00:27.229 --> 01:00:34.973
(B2) In L1, add a re-ranking stage that re-scores the fused top 20 by counting query concepts in each chunk.

788
01:00:35.253 --> 01:00:38.197
Does answer@5 rise at 25-word chunks?

789
01:00:38.477 --> 01:00:50.935
(B3) In L2, add chunked prefill (split prompts into 512-token pieces interleaved with decode steps) and measure TTFT p99 for long-context requests.

790
01:00:51.215 --> 01:00:57.807
(B3) In L3, add a 60-second TTL to cached stock answers with simulated stock changes.

791
01:00:58.087 --> 01:01:00.775
How many stale answers remain at each TTL?

792
01:01:02.125 --> 01:01:04.899
(B4) In L4, add a changeaddress tool.

793
01:01:05.179 --> 01:01:08.741
Which policy checks does it need, and what does the confirmation show?

794
01:01:09.021 --> 01:01:15.571
(B4) Design the eval pipeline that gates a model upgrade: datasets, metrics, thresholds and rollout.

